# CI Workflows — Triggers, Path Filters, and Skip Mechanisms

Date: 2026-04-27

---

## What Was Done

Added `paths:` filters to both GitHub Actions workflows so that pushes
which can't possibly affect a deliverable don't burn CI minutes
re-deploying the same artifact.

Workflows touched:

- `.github/workflows/deploy-client.yml`
- `.github/workflows/publish-server.yml`

---

## Triggers (per workflow)

### `deploy-client.yml` — GitHub Pages deploy

Fires on:

- `push` to `main` **and** at least one of these paths changed:
  - `apps/client/**`
  - `packages/shared/**` (client imports types/constants from here)
  - `pnpm-lock.yaml`, `pnpm-workspace.yaml`, `tsconfig.base.json`
  - `.github/workflows/deploy-client.yml` itself
- `workflow_dispatch` (manual run from the Actions UI — bypasses the path filter)

A README-only commit, a journal entry, a server-only change, or a
docker-compose tweak no longer redeploys the client.

### `publish-server.yml` — Docker Hub publish

Fires on:

- `push` of a tag matching `v*` **and** at least one of these paths changed:
  - `apps/server/**`
  - `packages/shared/**`
  - `package.json` (the root — `bump-version.mjs` writes here)
  - `pnpm-lock.yaml`, `pnpm-workspace.yaml`, `tsconfig.base.json`
  - `.github/workflows/publish-server.yml` itself
- `workflow_dispatch` (manual run, publishes the `:edge` tag)

The path filter is mostly defensive — tag pushes are already explicit
release events. But it means an accidental tag on a docs-only commit
won't re-publish a server image.

---

## Why path filters matter

Two properties:

1. **Save CI minutes.** GitHub free-tier minutes are finite. Path filters
   are the cheapest way to keep idle workflows from firing on pushes
   that don't matter (README edits, journal entries, docs).
2. **Self-document the workflow's surface.** The `paths:` block is a
   declarative answer to "what does this workflow depend on?" — easier
   to audit than reading the build steps and inferring it.

---

## Manual escape hatches

Two ways to skip workflows when you want to push something that *would*
match the path filter but you know doesn't need a build:

### `[skip ci]` in the commit subject

GitHub Actions special-cases commit messages containing any of:

```
[skip ci]   [ci skip]   [no ci]   [skip actions]   [actions skip]
```

When the head commit of a push has one of these in its subject or body,
**no workflows fire for that push** — even ones whose path filter would
otherwise match. Use this for one-off intentional skips:

```bash
git commit -m "docs: tweak README wording [no ci]"
```

### Manual re-run via `workflow_dispatch`

Both workflows keep `workflow_dispatch:` so you can trigger them by hand
from the Actions UI when you want to. This is useful when you skipped CI
on a commit but later realize you do want to ship it.

---

## What this does NOT do

- Path filters do not affect tag-push triggers in obvious ways. For
  `publish-server.yml`, GitHub evaluates `paths:` against the diff of
  the *commit being tagged*. If you tag a commit that only touched
  unrelated files, the workflow won't fire — even though tagging is
  usually an intentional release act. In practice, `pnpm version:bump`
  always touches files in the path filter (`packages/shared/src/version.ts`,
  `apps/server/package.json`, root `package.json`), so real release
  tags always run.
- Path filters do not retroactively cancel runs that are already queued.
  Skipping has to happen at trigger time.

---

## Triggering the configured pipelines today

| Action                                  | What fires                          |
|-----------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|
| Push to `main`, only `README.md` changed| Nothing.                            |
| Push to `main`, `apps/client/**` changed| `deploy-client.yml`.                |
| Push to `main`, `apps/server/**` changed| Nothing (server only ships on tag). |
| Push tag `v1.2.0` (any release commit)  | `publish-server.yml`.               |
| Any commit with `[no ci]` in subject    | Nothing.                            |
| Manual run from Actions UI              | The selected workflow.              |
